Documentation section docs/isbn.md#ISBNs > Validating:

`is_valid(isbn)` checks a normalised ISBN against its check digit. Only
thirteen-digit ISBNs are accepted — a ten-digit ISBN is always rejected, so
convert older ISBNs to thirteen digits before storing them.

The chunk bookshelf/isbn.py::is_valid before the change:

def is_valid(isbn):
    if len(isbn) != 13 or not isbn.isdigit():
        return False
    weighted = sum((int(digit) * (1 if position % 2 == 0 else 3) for position, digit in enumerate(isbn[:12])))
    return (10 - weighted % 10) % 10 == int(isbn[12])

And after it:

def is_valid(isbn):
    if len(isbn) == 10:
        return _valid_isbn10(isbn)
    if len(isbn) != 13 or not isbn.isdigit():
        return False
    weighted = sum((int(digit) * (1 if position % 2 == 0 else 3) for position, digit in enumerate(isbn[:12])))
    return (10 - weighted % 10) % 10 == int(isbn[12])

What the change made wrong: `is_valid(isbn)` now accepts valid 10-digit ISBNs instead of always rejecting them, so the section’s claim that only thirteen-digit ISBNs are accepted is no longer correct.

The section as the repair would leave it:

`is_valid(isbn)` checks a normalised ISBN against its check digit. Thirteen-digit ISBNs are accepted, and valid ten-digit ISBNs are also accepted, so convert older ISBNs to thirteen digits before storing them.
